 "drivingQuestion": "Why does the mishna cite an incident that contradicts its own ruling?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "The incident illustrates Shammai's dissenting stringency",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sukkah.28b.7",
   "daf": "28b",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "מַעֲשֶׂה וְיָלְדָה כַּלָּתוֹ כּוּ׳. מַעֲשֶׂה לִסְתּוֹר? חַסּוֹרֵי מְחַסְּרָא וְהָכִי קָתָנֵי: וְשַׁמַּאי מַחְמִיר, וּמַעֲשֶׂה נָמֵי וְיָלְדָה כַּלָּתוֹ שֶׁל שַׁמַּאי הַזָּקֵן, וּפִחֵת אֶת הַמַּעֲזִיבָה וְסִיכֵּךְ עַל הַמִּטָּה בִּשְׁבִיל הַקָּטָן.",
   "en": "The mishna relates: There was «an incident where the daughter-in-law of» Shammai the Elder «gave birth» and he removed part of the roof so the baby would be in a sukka. The Gemara asks: Does the mishna cite «an incident to contradict» the preceding halakha that minors that are not independent are exempt from the mitzva of sukka? The Gemara answers: The mishna is «incomplete, and it teaches the following: And Shammai is stringent» even with very small children; «and there was also an incident and the daughter-in-law of Shammai the Elder gave birth» and Shammai «removed the» coat of «plaster» from the roof and left the beams «and roofed» with the beams «over the bed for the» newborn «minor.»"
  }
 ],
